---
import UnitToggle from './UnitToggle.astro';

/* Three nav items. No Airlines index at launch — docs/15-sitemap.md.
 *
 * "CHECK A BAG" NAMED THE THING THIS SITE EXISTS TO PREVENT.
 *
 * Frank: "maybe there is a better name that describes what the page actually
 * does." It does — and the old label was worse than vague, because at an
 * airport "check a bag" means handing it over at the counter for the hold.
 * That is the outcome every page here is trying to help someone avoid, and it
 * was the most prominent link in the header.
 *
 * It also undersold the page and disagreed with it. The page is titled "Check
 * your whole trip" and its whole argument is that one leg being fine does not
 * mean the trip is — it takes an itinerary, names the leg that decides, and
 * draws the biggest bag that survives all of them. "Check a bag" describes a
 * single lookup against a single airline, which is the thing every competitor
 * does and the thing this page deliberately is not.
 *
 * "Check your trip" fixes all three: it cannot be misread as the hold, it
 * names the actual unit of work, and it is the page's own H1 shortened rather
 * than a second name for the same thing. A door and the room behind it should
 * say the same thing. */
const path = Astro.url.pathname;
const nav = [
  { href: '/bags/', label: 'Bags' },
  { href: '/guide/', label: 'Guide' },
  { href: '/check/', label: 'Check your trip' },
];
---
{/* TWO ROWS ON A PHONE, AND DELIBERATELY SO.
    This was one row at every width, and on a 375px screen the parts need
    about 476px: 48 for the wordmark, 225 for three labels and their gaps,
    139 for the unit toggle, plus 64 of gaps — against 327 once the page
    padding is off. There is no combination of smaller gaps that fits, so
    tightening them would only have moved the break.
    What actually happened was that "Check your trip" wrapped inside its own
    link, making the bar a ragged two lines while `h-16` still claimed it was
    64px tall. That is an accidental second row. This is an intentional one:
    wordmark and units on top, the three links beneath, back to a single row
    from 640px up. Nothing is hidden and no label is shortened — with three
    items a hamburger would cost a tap to reveal what already fits. */}
<header class="border-b border-rule bg-surface">
  <div
    class="mx-auto max-w-page px-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-8 gap-y-3 py-3
           sm:h-16 sm:flex-nowrap sm:py-0"
  >
    <a href="/" class="font-bold text-lg tracking-tight text-ink no-underline hover:text-blue">Sizer</a>

    {/* order-last + w-full drops the nav onto its own line below 640px. */}
    <nav class="order-last flex w-full gap-6 sm:order-none sm:mr-auto sm:w-auto">
      {nav.map((n) => (
        <a
          href={n.href}
          aria-current={path.startsWith(n.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
          class="whitespace-nowrap text-[0.9375rem] no-underline text-ink-2 hover:text-blue aria-[current=page]:text-ink aria-[current=page]:font-semibold"
        >{n.label}</a>
      ))}
    </nav>

    {/* ml-auto pins the toggle to the right of the top row on mobile; from
        sm: the nav's mr-auto does that job instead. */}
    <div class="ml-auto sm:ml-0"><UnitToggle /></div>
  </div>
</header>
